Electricity companies charge for energy in kilowatt-hours rather than in joules.
Explain what a kilowatt-hour is and why it is used, and describe how a householder could work out the cost of using an appliance for an evening.
Your answer should include:
• what a kilowatt-hour measures, and what it is in terms of a power and a time;
• how big a kilowatt-hour is compared with a joule, and why that makes it the better unit for a bill;
• the information needed from the appliance and the bill;
• the steps of the calculation, including any unit changes.
This question also assesses the quality of your written communication, including your use of specialist scientific terms.
